soc: mediatek: mt6797-a72: take the core rail through the regulator, and pick real OPPs

Two changes to the same rail, because they are the same mistake seen from
two sides.

1. VPROC2 IS NOW A REGULATOR, NOT AN i2c POKE.

The rail is declared in the DT and CONFIG_REGULATOR_DA9211=y, so mainline's
da9211 driver binds it at boot. This driver brought it up by writing the
DA9214 directly, behind the framework's back, so the framework saw a
regulator with no consumers -- and the core's unused-supply sweep switched
it off underneath a running cluster:

  [ 18.153957] mt6797-a72: A72 cluster at 1989 MHz on 1140 mV
  [ 31.712748] da9214_vproc2: disabling

The machine hung with no oops, because there was nothing left to oops with.
That sweep is regulator_init_complete_work, queued with a THIRTY SECOND
delay, so ANY A72 brought online inside that window died -- on every build
this project has ever made. It went unnoticed only because
gemini-a72-online fires at ~240 s, well clear of it. It surfaced the first
time a core was onlined by hand at 17 s.

regulator_get_optional() deliberately, not regulator_get(): the latter hands
back a dummy when the supply is missing, and a dummy accepts set_voltage and
enable without doing anything, so the rail would never come up and nothing
would say so. The matching proc-supply is added in dts/0060.

2. THE CLUSTER NOW RUNS AT A POINT THE VENDOR ACTUALLY SPECIFIES.

It ran at 2002 MHz on 1180 mV. 2002 MHz is in no vendor table, and 1180 mV
is the voltage for 2145 MHz -- two operating points above. So the cluster
held maximum voltage for a clock that never needed it, busy or idle, and
these are the two cores no frequency cap governs.

The default is now 1989 MHz on 1140 mV: 0.65% less clock for 40 mV less
rail, which is about 7% less core power since dynamic power goes as V^2.
Every point in the new table is at or better than the old fixed pair on both
axes.

That matters beyond tidiness. This pack measures 312 mOhm internal
resistance (105 discharge samples, 2026-08-27), so every extra amp costs
310 mV of headroom, and the device browned out 71 seconds after being
unplugged with these cores at maximum voltage.

The table is the vendor's own for cluster B (04-docs/A72-OPP-TABLE, from
ubports-3.18 mt_cpufreq.c), FY1221 / CPU_LEVEL_0 -- the lowest-clocked of
the six bins and so the highest voltage for any clock, which under-clocks a
faster die but can never over-volt. It stops at 793 MHz because
mt6797_a72_retune() refuses anything below 750.

The sysfs knob now moves the rail with the clock instead of leaving it
wherever it was. Requests above the top OPP still work, on the top voltage,
because the documented 2496 MHz burst depends on it -- flagged FIXME, since
the vendor publishes no voltage up there and none is invented here.

WHAT THIS IS NOT: a cpufreq driver. Linux allows exactly one
cpufreq_driver system-wide and mtk-cpufreq already owns it, so registering
a second returns -EEXIST (measured, build #97). The way in is the driver
already there, which declines these cores for one reason:

  mtk-cpufreq: cpu8: no operating-points-v2, not managing it

so governing this cluster is a matter of describing it. It wants clocks
named "cpu" and "intermediate", a "proc" supply, and operating-points-v2.
This change and dts/0060 supply the second of the three.
